# gin-kit development

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing generated code. Keep the explicit
router → handler → service → repository → database flow, pass
`context.Context` through every boundary, and keep transport shapes in
`internal/dto`.
{{if eq .ProjectType "runtime"}}
This project consumes the versioned `github.com/Alfian57/gin-kit/runtime`
runtime; customize it through public options, hooks, middleware, and
interfaces — never copy or edit gin-kit core.
{{else}}
This project vendors its runtime under `internal/platform/`; you may edit it
directly, but keep the HTTP contract (envelope, error codes, validation
shape) stable.
{{end}}
## Add a resource end-to-end

```bash
gin-kit generate resource Invoice --fields "number:string,amount:float64,paid:bool,note:text?"
```

Then: paste the printed wiring snippet into `internal/app/app.go`, run
`gin-kit db up` for its migration, and `gin-kit routes` to confirm the
routes. The generator produced the domain model, DTOs, repository, service,
handler, factory, tests, and migration — nothing else needs hand-writing.
To evolve it, edit the generated files directly (they are yours).

## Add a custom validation rule

{{if eq .ProjectType "runtime"}}Register on the application validator during wiring, before `runtime.New`:

```go
v := validation.New()
_ = v.RegisterRule("slug", func(fl validator.FieldLevel) bool { ... })
v.RegisterMessage("slug", "The {field} field must not contain spaces.")
// runtime.Options{Validator: v}
```
{{else}}Register on `validation.Default` during startup (`internal/app/app.go`):

```go
_ = validation.Default.RegisterRule("slug", func(fl validator.FieldLevel) bool { ... })
validation.Default.RegisterMessage("slug", "The {field} field must not contain spaces.")
```
{{end}}
Then use `validate:"slug"` in request DTOs — every `httpx.BindJSON/Query/URI`
call enforces it and renders the message with field details.
{{if eq .ProjectType "runtime"}}
## Background work

```bash
gin-kit generate job SendReceipt    # typed payload + handler + registration snippet
gin-kit generate event InvoicePaid  # typed event + listener
gin-kit generate mail ReceiptMail   # mailable + HTML template
```

The sync queue driver runs jobs inline in development; set
`QUEUE_DRIVER=redis` + `REDIS_URL` in production. Schedule recurring work
with `schedule.New` + `app.Go("scheduler", scheduler.Run)`.
{{end}}
## Testing

- Handler tests drive real HTTP through the router with in-memory repository
  fakes (see any generated `*_handler_test.go`).
- Factories (`internal/database/factories/`) build realistic test data;
  `.Seeded(n)` makes them deterministic.
{{if eq .ProjectType "runtime"}}- `runtime/apptest` boots the full application for integration tests
  (`apptest.New`, `OpenSQLite`, `Migrate`, `Seed`); `runtime/browsertest`
  adds Playwright browser tests{{if eq .Mode "ui"}} (see `e2e/`){{end}}.
{{end}}- Run everything with `gin-kit check` (read-only: gofmt report, tests, vet).

## Database workflow

```bash
gin-kit db up            # apply migrations
gin-kit db create <name> # new empty migration (versioned SQL — never AutoMigrate)
gin-kit db seed          # run the explicit seeder registry
gin-kit db fresh --yes   # drop + migrate + seed (destructive)
```

Seeders and factories are registries you edit by hand; keep seeders
idempotent.

## Safety defaults

Keep request IDs, security headers, trusted proxy configuration, body
limits, readiness checks, and rate-limit middleware enabled. Never trust
forwarded client IP headers without configuring `TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS`.
Never put secrets in source files or migrations — configuration lives in
`.env` (local) and the environment (deployed); update `.env.example` when
you add a variable.

## Troubleshooting

`gin-kit doctor` diagnoses toolchain, manifest, and database issues.
`gin-kit explain <topic>` answers architecture questions offline
(`architecture`, `request-flow`, `database`, `auth`, `commands`). Every
error response carries a `request_id` — correlate it with the structured
logs.
